# gitleaks ignore list: one fingerprint per VERIFIED false positive.
#
# secret-scan.yml runs `gitleaks detect --no-git`, which reads this file from the
# scan root. In that mode a fingerprint is `<file>:<rule-id>:<line>` and silences
# exactly one finding: the same rule on any other line, any other rule on the same
# line, and every other file are still scanned. Nothing here is a path or a rule.
#
# The line number is part of the fingerprint, so an edit that moves a listed line
# brings its finding back at the new line and the scan goes red again. When that
# happens, check the value is still the same non-secret, then update the line
# number here. Never swap an entry for a path or rule-wide exemption. The exact
# fingerprint is printed in the CI log, or locally by:
#   gitleaks detect --no-git --source . --config .gitleaks.toml --redact --verbose
#
# A real credential never goes in this file: take it out of the code and rotate it.

# bot/shared/services/child-test/sa/build.js:428 (generic-api-key)
# `scriptKey: 'ma.f...'` is a task id, the same string as the `task:` id three lines
# up, used to look up that task's lines in the script table. The rule fires on the
# word "Key" followed by a quoted value.
bot/shared/services/child-test/sa/build.js:generic-api-key:428

# portal/src/portal/teacher/notices/RemindCard.test.tsx:58 (generic-api-key)
# Test fixture: the mocked response of GET /api/wqpush/key in a unit test. The
# value is base64url for a short ASCII string that begins "fake", and what it
# stands in for is the VAPID public key, which every browser is sent anyway.
# (The paths allowlist in .gitleaks.toml covers *.test.js only, not *.test.tsx.
# It is not widened here: one fingerprint is narrower than every TypeScript test.)
portal/src/portal/teacher/notices/RemindCard.test.tsx:generic-api-key:58

